Step 1: Create the Flutter project
Run the following commands in the terminal:

bash
flutter create flutter_lab
cd flutter_lab
Step 2: Create the UI component
Open lib/main.dart and replace its contents with the following code.

dart

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(const MaterialApp(
    home: CounterPage(),
    debugShowCheckedModeBanner: false,
  ));
}

class CounterPage extends StatefulWidget {
  const CounterPage({super.key});

  @override
  State<CounterPage> createState() => _CounterPageState();
}

class _CounterPageState extends State<CounterPage> {
  int count = 0;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: const Text('Flutter Counter'),
      ),
      body: Center(
        child: Column(
          mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.center,
          children: [
            Text(
              'Count: $count',
              key: const Key('counterText'),
              style: const TextStyle(fontSize: 24),
            ),
            const SizedBox(height: 20),
            ElevatedButton(
              key: const Key('incrementButton'),
              onPressed: () {
                setState(() {
                  count++;
                });
              },
              child: const Text('Increment'),
            ),
          ],
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
Step 3: Write the widget test
Create a file named test/widget_test.dart and replace its contents with the following code.

dart

import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';
import 'package:flutter_lab/main.dart';

void main() {
  testWidgets('Counter increments when button is tapped',
      (WidgetTester tester) async {
    await tester.pumpWidget(const CounterPage());

    // Check the initial count.
    expect(find.text('Count: 0'), findsOneWidget);

    // Tap the increment button.
    await tester.tap(find.byKey(const Key('incrementButton')));
    await tester.pump();

    // Verify that the count has increased.
    expect(find.text('Count: 1'), findsOneWidget);
  });

  testWidgets('Counter increments twice',
      (WidgetTester tester) async {
    await tester.pumpWidget(const CounterPage());

    await tester.tap(find.byKey(const Key('incrementButton')));
    await tester.pump();

    await tester.tap(find.byKey(const Key('incrementButton')));
    await tester.pump();

    expect(find.text('Count: 2'), findsOneWidget);
  });
}
Note: Add the following import to the test file because it uses Key:

dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
The complete set of imports should therefore include both flutter/material.dart and flutter_test/flutter_test.dart.

Also, flutter_lab must match the project name in pubspec.yaml.

Step 4: Execute the test
Run this command:

bash
flutter test
Expected output:

text
00:00 +0: Counter increments when button is tapped
00:00 +1: Counter increments twice
00:00 +2: All tests passed!
The exact timing and test descriptions may vary by Flutter version.

b) Using Flutter Debugging Tools
Step 1: Analyze the application
bash
flutter analyze
This command identifies potential errors, warnings, and coding issues.

Step 2: Run the application in debug mode
bash
flutter run
The application launches with debugging support enabled.

